# Twelve principles of animation

<small>Genuary 2026, day 02 — prompt by Anna Lucia</small>

<VideoLoop src="/02/loop.mp4" poster="/02/poster.jpg" label="Pastel softbody blobs launched into the air, squashing and stretching as they land" />

Squash and stretch, the first principle of animation, done by a physics engine
instead of an animator. Noisy circular blobs become soft bodies: each outline
point is a small JBox2D circle, chained to its neighbors with spring joints and
cross-braced with softer diagonal springs so the blob holds its shape — barely.
Launched upwards, they arc (anticipation! follow-through!), splat on the floor
and jiggle themselves back to rest.

In the interactive sketch you can also draw your own shapes point by point,
drag blobs around and throw them; the clip above is the scripted version that
just keeps pressing the "Create Blob" button.

```kotlin
fun generateBlob(): List<Vector2> {
    val points = mutableListOf<Vector2>()
    for (i in 0 until blobSegments - 1) {
        val angle = (i.toDouble() / (blobSegments - 1)) * 2.0 * PI
        // sample simplex noise on a circle for a smooth, closed deformation
        val noiseValue = simplex(noiseSeed, Vector2(cos(angle) * 5.0, sin(angle) * 5.0))
        val radius = blobRadius + noiseValue * blobNoiseFactor
        points.add(Vector2(centerX + cos(angle) * radius, centerY + sin(angle) * radius))
    }
    return points
}
```

